<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guyana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Co-operative Republic of Guyana, British Guiana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guyana, officially the Co-operative Republic of Guyana, is a country on the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and Venezuela. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were the first Europeans to found colonies there, among them Essequibo (1616) and Berbice (1627); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took control in 1796 and received the Dutch colonies in 1814; in 1831 they were united as British Guiana. The country became independent from the United Kingdom on 26 May 1966 and a republic on 23 February 1970. Venezuela has claimed the land west of the Essequibo River since its own independence in 1824.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guyana lies between latitudes 1° and 9° N and covers 214,969 square kilometres, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> to the south and south-west, and Venezuela to the west. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guianas">Guianas</a>, the region of the Guiana Shield between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, whose name is said to come from an Indigenous language and to mean land of many waters.</p><p>The country divides into a narrow marshy coastal plain, where most people live, a white sand belt inland that holds most of the mineral deposits, dense forest in the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> in the south-west and interior highlands rising towards the Brazilian border.</p><p>The area is rich in wildlife, with more than 225 species of mammals and about 900 of birds recorded, among them the arapaima, the giant otter and the hoatzin, the national bird.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nine Indigenous peoples live in Guyana today: the Wai Wai, Macushi, Patamona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokono">Lokono</a>, Kalina, Wapishana, Pemon, Akawaio and Warao. Historians suppose that the Lokono and Kalina came from the South American interior and moved north, first to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guianas">Guianas</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> sighted the coast on his third voyage in 1498, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Sir Walter Raleigh</a> wrote an account in 1596. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlements followed at Pomeroon in 1581, Essequibo in 1616, Berbice in 1627 and Demerara in 1752. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> assumed control in 1796, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and the Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1814 ceded Demerara-Essequibo and Berbice.</p><p>Under British rule the economy rested largely on plantations, and primary education became compulsory in 1876. Independence in 1966 was followed within months by a Venezuelan crossing of the border in October 1966, when troops seized Ankoko Island and built installations and an airstrip there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital and largest city is Georgetown. English is the official language, and most people speak Guyanese Creole, an English-based creole; Indigenous Cariban languages are spoken by a small minority. About 744,000 people live in the country, roughly 90 per cent of them on a coastal strip 16 to 64 kilometres wide. In 2012 the population was 64 per cent Christian, 25 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and 7 per cent Muslim.</p><p>Guyana hosts the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean-community">Caribbean Community</a> and was a founding member of the Union of South American Nations in 2008. Venezuela's claim to the land west of the Essequibo was taken by Guyana to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> in 2018, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> disputes the area east of the Corentyne River.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guyana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Venezuela has fought no major foreign war since independence, but it maintains a long-standing claim to the Essequibo region administered by <b>Guyana</b>, which an arbitral award of 1899 had rejected.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Indentured labourers taken from India by the British in the 19th century founded Hindu communities in Mauritius, Fiji, Trinidad, <b>Guyana</b> and South Africa, and migration in the 20th century carried Hinduism to Britain, North America and the Gulf.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Migrants of the colonial era took it to Malaysia, Singapore, Mauritius, South Africa, Burma and other lands; in Réunion, <b>Guyana</b>, Fiji and Trinidad many have Tamil origins but few speak it, and in Réunion, where France forbade it in public, it is now being learnt again.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Guyana</b> marks 23 February as Republic Day in memory of the Berbice Rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-23">23 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of 1812 the sloop of war USS Hornet fought the brig-sloop HMS Peacock off the mouth of the Demerara River in <b>Guyana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-24">24 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
